  "rationale": "The requirement asks for one structural item. It is a method in `impl Sources` with the signature `pub async fn list(&self, scope: &SourceScope) -> Result<Vec<PathBuf>, SourceError>`.\n\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/sources/public.rs#impl[Sources]/fn[list]` matches that signature. It is public and async, and it takes `&self` and `&SourceScope`. It returns `Result<Vec<PathBuf>, SourceError>`.\n- Its body delegates to `self.analysis.provider.list(scope)`. The provider is a `LocalSources`, and `crates/trust-but-verify/src/sources.rs#impl[LocalSources/SourceProvider]` sets `type Error = SourceError`. Its `list` returns `Vec<ResourceId>`, which the method maps to `PathBuf` through `id.location`. The `?` therefore needs no error conversion.\n- `SourceScope` and `SourceError` are both defined in `crates/trust-but-verify/src/sources/public.rs`. `SourceScope` is a struct and `SourceError` is an enum, so the named types exist.\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/sources.rs#trait[SourceProvider]/fn[list]` is the internal trait method that `Sources::list` calls. Its return type differs from the public one, but it is a helper reached from the marked root. The extra {impl} mark on it does no harm.\n\nI found no structural gap, no ambiguity, and no {fixme} acknowledgments.",
